success: true
exit_code: 0
----- stdout -----
Initialize NAILS directory structure on a hidden volume

Usage: nails init [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          Path to the mounted hidden volume to initialize

Options:
      --allow-unmounted
          Initialize a path that is not a mounted volume of its own

          By default `init` refuses a target that shares a device with `/`, because writing the NAILS layout onto the decoy disk is exactly the mistake that leaves an identifying tree where the real volume later hides it. Pass this for a directory-style backend that is deliberately not a separate mount.

  -c, --config <PATH>
          Path to the configuration file (overrides binary-relative discovery)

      --hardware-config <PATH>
          Base hardware configuration to seed the hidden NixOS layout from

          Overrides the default `/etc/nixos/hardware-configuration.nix`. On a non-NixOS host the layout is skipped unless this is given.

  -v, --verbose...
          More diagnostics on stderr: -v for debug, -vv for trace

      --no-hardware-config
          Do not create the hidden NixOS layout at all

  -q, --quiet
          Print only errors and the final result

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a stream only when it is a terminal, TERM is not `dumb` and NO_COLOR is unset or empty
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour

          [default: auto]

      --ascii
          Use ASCII markers instead of Unicode symbols and box drawing

      --json
          Print machine-readable JSON on stdout

      --no-log-file
          Do not write the log file on the hidden volume (diagnostics still go to stderr)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

EXAMPLES:
  nails init /mnt/data                Lay out the hidden volume at /mnt/data
  nails init /mnt/data --no-hardware-config   Skip the hidden NixOS layout

----- stderr -----
